  "response": "**File size**: 260,000 bytes (253.9 KB) — just over the 200 KB low-severity threshold; not near medium (500 KB) or high (1000 KB).\n\n**Verdict**: Request changes, don't block. Severity is `low` with `agent_risk: 0.52`, effort is `quick`, and the fix has no behavioural risk. A 254 KB hero is wasteful but not a release blocker — the PR can land once the asset is right-sized.\n\n**Recommended transformation**:\n\n- **Target dimensions**: a landing-page hero typically renders at ~1200–1600 CSS px wide and ~500–700 px tall. Encode at **2× the rendered size** (around **2400×900 to 3200×1200**) so it stays crisp on Retina, then serve smaller variants via `srcset` for narrower viewports (e.g. 800w, 1200w, 1600w, 2400w). Adjust to the exact rendered box once you know the design's hero height — there's no reason to ship pixels beyond `max-width × 2`.\n\n- **Format**: **AVIF, with a WebP fallback** in a `<picture>` element. For a photographic hero, AVIF typically lands 30–50 % smaller than WebP at equivalent perceptual quality; on a 254 KB PNG that's usually a drop to ~40–70 KB AVIF (vs ~80–110 KB WebP). Browser support for AVIF is broad as of 2026 (Safari 16+, Chrome 85+, Firefox 93+), so it's safe as the primary format — the WebP source in the `<picture>` covers older clients, and the PNG can be dropped entirely once the fallback is in place.\n\nSuggested `<picture>` shape:\n\n```html\n<picture>\n  <source type=\"image/avif\" srcset=\"hero-800.avif 800w, hero-1600.avif 1600w, hero-2400.avif 2400w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"hero-800.webp 800w, hero-1600.webp 1600w, hero-2400.webp 2400w\" sizes=\"100vw\">\n  <img src=\"hero-1600.webp\" alt=\"…\" width=\"1600\" height=\"600\">\n</picture>\n```",
